All the (small) changes outside end.c are just the declaration for the
interresting function calculate_score() and the #score wiz command.

In end.c, I conditionally replace old-style scoring with
calculate_score() which scores:

(a)
  - attributes, intrinsics, etc
  - objects and species that are known

(b)
  - conducts

(c)
  - species that
    . have had one member killed
    . have been genocided
    . are extinct
    . have been eradicated (extinct /and/ all dead)
  - game events and places reached
  - feats (all 0 of them)
  - unique loot

Scoring for (a) ramps up from 0-10000 turns (to avoid starting
equipment and stats to give score to early deaths; ideally I want to
save the score at gamestart and substract it at game end instead but
that will require changing savegame file format).

Scoring for (b) kicks in at 10000 then ramps up to 20000 (conducts are
meaninglss at the start of the game.  After all, everyone starts
foodless-- it's *staying* foodless that's hard).

Note that everything on this list is finite:  either you have it or
you don't; you cannot increase your score by doing something more than
once (as opposed to just once).  Some are longer do to, however. 
Except for the Nazgul, eradication is long and difficult (but only
counts once per race).

The score up to that point is scaled by the role factor (see my other
message about how I'm going to change that).

After that, the time penalty is applied if the game is past 10000
moves:  Every tenth move beyond the /score/ first moves substracts one
point, up to 3/4 of your score.  So expending time to raise your score
is only worth it to a point after which you get increasingly
diminishing returns.

Finally, if the game is over, the whole score is scaled according to
how it ended (ascention being the highest scoring, quitting the
lowest, with various demises inbetween (the more YASD the higher--
death is merciless but not without a sense of humor)).

